          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about diet?",
          "answer": "For managing high blood pressure and generally promoting health, a dietary pattern that emphasizes vegetables, fruits, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts, and vegetable oils is often recommended. This approach also suggests limiting saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets, and sugar-sweetened drinks (lifestyle-hypertension-diet).\n\nIt's important to understand that this is about an overall eating pattern rather than focusing on a single \"blood pressure food.\" Reducing dietary sodium is also a key component, and most of the sodium we consume comes from packaged and restaurant foods rather than from salt added at the table (lifestyle-hypertension-diet, lifestyle-heart-failure-diet).\n\nGeneral healthy eating patterns, such as those recommended for type 2 diabetes, also share common principles: more non-starchy vegetables, whole grains instead of refined grains, legumes, nuts, and minimally processed foods. They also suggest fewer refined grains, added sugars, sugar-sweetened drinks, and highly processed foods (lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet). The most effective diet is often one that a person can maintain long-term, adapted to their individual preferences, culture, budget, and cooking situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
